---
/* /people/<slug>/ - ONE PAGE FOR EVERY PERSON THIS ARCHIVE NAMES, DRAWN BY THE KIT'S SHARED PEOPLE PAGE
   (PERSON-PAGE-SPEC.md). Pilot on this archive, 17 October 2026 (the second, after Luwinski).

   WHAT THE KIT DECIDES NOW, and this page no longer does: the order and the words of the headings
   (Family, What the record says, Where this comes from, Others of the same name, Photographs and papers,
   Notes and corrections), the chart's source words and legend, how a date is written, what a living
   relative looks like, and what a living person's page may hold. THE LIVING STANDARD (version 2) is the
   kit's: a living person is named with their relationships and their town and country and nothing more;
   their page is `noindex`, has no structured data, no table, no image, no year, and the kit's PersonPage
   does not render a record, a source, a note or a photograph for them at all. tools/check_living_v2.py
   reads the built pages and still fails on any of it.

   WHO IS LIVING is not decided here. tools/build.py asks tools/living_v2.py (the kit's living_rule.py) and
   writes `living` into people.json; src/lib/personpage.js hands that answer to the kit's JavaScript rule
   as `saidLiving`, for those people only, and checks at build time that the rule agrees with the generator
   about everybody else. No birth year is ever supplied that the data does not hold.

   THIS ARCHIVE MARKS THE PERSON, NOT THE LINK, so the chart is drawn with edgeLevel={false}: its caption says
   that each person is marked by what is known of them. «A record has been read about them» is `read`, every
   other relative is `tree`, and an ancestor of the two children is on the direct line (a bar, not a source).

   WHAT THE KIT'S CHART CANNOT SAY, said under it instead of dropped: the count of relationships proved by a
   record, two children who share a name, the names this archive refuses to merge, and the edges a record
   contradicts. They are the archive's own prose, moved into the chart's notes slot unchanged.

   WHAT IS THIS ARCHIVE'S OWN: «Why this person is here», «The burial» and «The crossing» are drawn after the
   six shared sections as kind="more" (the page labels them «this archive only»).

   WHY THIS ROUTE CARRIES KIT COMPONENTS. `people/[slug]` and `people/index` are page names other archives
   also have, and checkpages counts a shared page name as bespoke if the page uses NO kit component at all;
   this archive's cap is zero. */
import { md } from "@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/md.js";
import PS from "@daviddef/archive-kit/data/public-sources.json";
import { personMeta, stubReason } from "@daviddef/archive-kit/lib/person.js";
import PersonPage from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/PersonPage.astro";
import PersonSection from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/PersonSection.astro";
import "@daviddef/archive-kit/styles/persontree.css";
import "@daviddef/archive-kit/styles/personpage.css";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../../lib/url.js";
import { people, norm } from "../../lib/people.js";
import { kitPerson, agreeWithGenerator } from "../../lib/personpage.js";
import disputed from "../../data/disputed.json";
import nudgee from "../../data/nudgee.json";
import ships from "../../data/ships.json";
import components from "../../data/components.json";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  /* Everyone in the tree gets a page. The living standard (v2) decides what a living person's page holds;
     dates for anyone 80 or younger are absent from the data rather than hidden here. See /method. */
  return people.map((p) => ({ params: { slug: p.slug }, props: { p } }));
}
const { p } = Astro.props;

const grave = p.living ? null : nudgee.burials.find((b) => b.slug === p.slug);
const fixes = p.living ? [] : nudgee.corrections.filter((c) => c.slug === p.slug);
const ship = p.living ? null : ships.find((s) => s.slug === p.slug);
const comp = components.find((c) => c.comp === p.comp);
const sameName = people.filter((q) => q.slug !== p.slug && norm(q.name) === norm(p.name));
const edges = ["parents", "spouses", "children", "siblings"].flatMap((b) => p[b] || []);
/* «read» is a register act, «index» an index entry; both are a record. «tree»
   is Michael's assertion, and a sibling edge carries no via at all. */
const provedEdges = edges.filter((e) => e.via === "read" || e.via === "index").length;
const asserted = edges.length - provedEdges;
/* A LIVING PERSON: NAME, RELATIONSHIPS, TOWN AND COUNTRY, AND NOTHING MORE
   (David, 16 October 2026; the estate's living-people standard v2). The data for a living person is built
   from name, relationships and town and country only (tools/build.py record()): there is nothing else in
   people.json for this page to leave out, and the kit is the second lock, not the first. An undated person
   with a living parent is not published at all: there is no page for them and they are nobody's relative. */
const living = !!p.living;
const home = p.birthPlace || p.deathPlace || p.burialPlace || "";
const EV = { born: "Born", died: "Died", buried: "Buried", baptised: "Baptised",
             christened: "Christened", lived: "Lived", arrival: "Arrived", departure: "Left" };

/* The one sentence under the name, which is also the description. It is the line this page has always had
   under its name; a living person gets the standard's fixed sentence from the kit instead. */
const dek = p.ancGen === 0 || p.onSpine
  ? (p.ancGen ? `An ancestor of Mia and Rocco — generation ${p.ancGen} above them.`
              : "One of the two children this archive is for.")
  : home ? `Of ${home}.`
  : edges.length ? "Known from the family tree. No record has been read for this person."
  : "Named in this archive, with no relationship recorded for them yet.";

const person = kitPerson(p, { lede: dek });
agreeWithGenerator(p, person);
const meta = personMeta(person, { siteName: "", sources: PS });
/* PersonPage draws the stub sentence only when NO `record` slot is passed, and Astro counts a slot as passed
   even when its condition is false, so a conditional slot would leave the heading with nothing under it.
   The slot is therefore always passed and says the same two things the kit says (PersonPage.astro). */
const stubNote = "the family tree that names them";
const stub = living ? null : stubReason(person);

/* The register's own words, kept in the record section wherever the header could not print a date: a bound
   («before 7 January 1834»), a date with a place after it, a statement that someone was alive. */
const lifeLine = [p.bornText ? `Born ${p.bornText}` : "", p.diedText ? `died ${p.diedText}` : ""].filter(Boolean).join(" · ");
const lossy = !living && ((p.bornText && !person.born) || (p.diedText && !person.died));

const events = (p.events || []).filter((e) => e.place || (e.what !== "lived" && e.date));
const hasCorrections = !living && p.corrections && p.corrections.length > 0;
const hasRecord = !living && (events.length > 0 || lossy || hasCorrections);
const dis = living ? {} : (disputed[p.slug] || {});
const nothing = !edges.length;

/* Names repeat relentlessly in these three towns, and the archive refuses to
   merge on a name. Where two people in one row answer to the same name, say so
   — it is usually a dead child's name given again, and it is also exactly how
   a records-matcher invents one person out of two. */
const clash = (rows) => {
  const seen = {};
  for (const r of rows) seen[r.name] = (seen[r.name] || 0) + 1;
  return Object.entries(seen).filter(([, n]) => n > 1).map(([n]) => n);
};
const clashChildren = clash(p.children || []);
const clashOther = [...new Set([...clash(p.siblings || []), ...clash(p.parents || []), ...clash(p.spouses || [])])];
const palette = { bg: "var(--panel)", "node-bg": "var(--ground)", line: "var(--terra)",
                  tree: "var(--ink-3)", via: "var(--ink-3)", gen: "var(--terra)" };
---
<Base title={meta.title} dek={meta.description} noindex={meta.noindex} person={meta.about ?? undefined} ownHeading>

  <PersonPage person={person} peopleHref={u("/people/")} edgeLevel={false} palette={palette}
              stubNote={stubNote}>

    <Fragment slot="chart-notes">
      {nothing && (
        <p class="warn">No family record in this archive names this person &mdash; not as a
          child, not as a spouse, not as a parent. They are held here because the
          tree holds them, and no relationship of theirs is recorded to draw.</p>
      )}
      {/* "Every relationship drawn here" reads as a boast about nothing when none is
          drawn, and it is the same fault as the empty chart: a sentence about a set
          that happens to be empty. */}
      {!nothing && provedEdges === 0 && <p class="warn"><strong>Every relationship drawn here comes from the
      family tree, and not one of them has yet been proved by a record.</strong> That is the honest
      state of this archive for this person: the chart shows what Michael Mazza's tree asserts, and the
      colour tells you only whether anything at all has been read about the person at the other end.
      <a href={u("/method/")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>}
      {!nothing && provedEdges > 0 && asserted > 0 && <p class="warn"><strong>{provedEdges} of the
      {" " + edges.length} relationships drawn here have been proved by a record</strong>; the other
      {" " + asserted} come from Michael Mazza's family tree and have not. The colour on each edge says
      which is which, and it is drawn per edge so that one proved link cannot vouch for the rest.
      <a href={u("/method/")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>}
      {!nothing && provedEdges > 0 && asserted === 0 && <p class="warn"><strong>Every relationship drawn
      here has been proved by a record</strong> — {edges.length === 1 ? "the one edge on this chart was"
      : `all ${edges.length} edges on this chart were`} read out of a register rather than taken from
      the family tree. <a href={u("/method/")}>The method &rarr;</a></p>}
      {clashChildren.length ? (
        <p class="warn"><strong>Two children here carry one name</strong> — {clashChildren.join(", ")}.
        In these towns that is usually not an error: <strong>a child who died was very often replaced
        by a later child given the same name.</strong> It is also exactly how a records-matcher invents
        one person out of two, which is why they are kept apart here and the reader is told.</p>
      ) : null}
      {clashOther.length ? (
        <p class="warn"><strong>More than one person in this row carries the same name</strong> —
        {" " + clashOther.join(", ")}. That is what the source says. <strong>This archive does not merge
        records on a name</strong>, so they are drawn separately even where one duplicated entry is the
        likelier explanation. <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The 58 names it refuses to merge →</a></p>
      ) : null}
    </Fragment>

    {Object.keys(dis).length ? (
      <figcaption class="disputed-note" slot="after">
        <strong>One or more of the lines above is contradicted by a record this archive has read.</strong>
        <ul>
          {Object.entries(dis).map(([slug, d]) => (
            <li><a href={u("/people/" + slug)}>{slug.replace(/-/g, " ")}</a> — {d.why}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p>The chart still draws the family tree exactly as it was given. This archive does not
        silently rewrite someone else's research — it marks the edge and shows its working.</p>
      </figcaption>
    ) : null}

    <Fragment slot="record">
      {hasRecord ? (<>
      {lossy && <p class="small"><strong>{lifeLine}</strong></p>}
      {events.length ? (
        <div class="rec">
          {events.map((e) => (<>
            <div class="k">{EV[e.what] || e.what}</div>
            <div class="v">{e.place || "—"}</div>
            {/* e.text is the register's own wording where the date is a bound:
                «before 7 October 1872» is what the act supports, and 1872 is
                not. e.date is empty for those by design — see
                tools/register_source.py. */}
            <div class="w">{e.date || e.text || ""}</div>
          </>))}
        </div>
      ) : null}
      {/* A reader who skims stops at the block above, and the block above is the
          TREE's account. Where a register contradicts it the correction is
          further down the page, and Maria Nicotra is the case that showed this
          matters: her line here still reads «Piedimonte Etneo, 1875» while the
          act found in September gives Mascali, 8 December 1875, to the hour.
          The fix is a pointer, not a rewrite — the archive does not overwrite
          what it was given. It cannot say WHICH line is disputed, because
          corrections.tsv carries prose and not a field name, and guessing which
          fact a paragraph is about is exactly the kind of inference this archive
          refuses. So it says that one of them is, and where to look. */}
      {hasCorrections ? (
        <p class="small">
          <strong>A register contradicts what is written above.</strong> The lines in this block are
          the family tree's account. {p.corrections.length === 1 ? "One reading" : `${p.corrections.length} readings`}
          {" "}taken from the original acts {p.corrections.length === 1 ? "disagrees" : "disagree"} with
          it — <a href="#disputed">what the registers say →</a>
        </p>
      ) : null}
      </>) : stub
        ? <p class="pnone pstub">Known only from {stubNote}; nothing else is recorded yet.</p>
        : <p class="pnone">No event is recorded for this person in this archive.</p>}
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="sources">
      {p.fromRegister && (
        <p class="small"><strong>This person is not in the family's
        file.</strong> They exist here because an act names them, and this archive has read it.
        {p.trade ? ` The register gives their trade as ${p.trade}.` : ""}
        {(p.registerSources || []).length > 0 && (<>
          {" "}The reading is <strong>{p.registerSources.join("; ")}</strong>.</>)}
        {" "}Nothing on this page comes from Michael's export, which never held them — so there is no
        tree account here to agree or disagree with, and the
        <a href={u("/components/")}> pieces the export arrived in</a> do not include them.
        <a href={u("/ancestors/")}> How many there are →</a></p>
      )}
      {p.comp !== 1 && comp && (
        <p class="small">This person is in
        <strong> component {p.comp}</strong> of the export — a group of {comp.n}
        {comp.towns.length ? ` mostly of ${comp.towns[0].name}` : ""} that
        <strong> no record joins</strong> to the line reaching Mia and Rocco.
        They are very probably kin. The archive does not yet say how.
        <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Why →</a></p>
      )}
      <p class="small">
        {/* COUNTED, because this sentence was false on 660 of these pages and on
            Salvatore Mazza's it contradicted the chart directly above it: the
            chart said «4 of the 8 relationships drawn here have been proved by a
            record» and this paragraph said none of them had. Same fault as
            PersonTree.astro's version, fixed there first and still living here,
            because the claim was written twice and only one copy was counted. */}
        {edges.length
          ? provedEdges === 0
            ? <span><strong>All {edges.length}</strong> of the relationships drawn above come from
              <strong> Michael Mazza's family tree</strong>, and none of them has been proved by a
              record this archive has read.</span>
            : provedEdges === edges.length
              ? <span><strong>All {edges.length}</strong> of the relationships drawn above have been
                <strong> proved by a record</strong> this archive has read, rather than taken from
                the family tree.</span>
              : <span><strong>{provedEdges} of the {edges.length}</strong> relationships drawn above
                have been <strong>proved by a record</strong> this archive has read. The other
                {" " + asserted} come from
                <strong> Michael Mazza's family tree</strong> and have not.</span>
          : <span>No relationship is recorded for this person.</span>}
      </p>
      {p.records && p.records.length ? (
        <>
          <p class="small"><strong>{p.records.length} record{p.records.length === 1 ? " has" : "s have"} been
          read about them:</strong></p>
          <ul class="small" style="margin:.4rem 0 0;padding-left:18px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--ink-2)">
            {p.records.map((r) => (
              <li><a href={u(r.href)}>{r.what}</a> <span style="color:var(--ink-3)">— {r.kind}</span></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p class="small"><strong>No record has been read for this person.</strong>
        {" "}What is known of them is what the tree asserts, and it is <strong>unverified</strong>.</p>
      )}
      {p.onSpine && (
        <p class="small"><a href={u("/ancestors/")}>See this generation on the ancestors page →</a></p>
      )}
      <p class="small">{p.comp != null && <><strong>Component {p.comp}</strong> ·</>}
        <a href={u("/people/") + "?q=" + encodeURIComponent(p.surname)}> every {p.surname || "person"} in the archive →</a></p>
    </Fragment>

    {/* The sections below are optional, and Astro counts a conditional named slot as passed even when its
        condition is false (an empty heading, a claim made in silence), so they are PersonSection elements in
        the default slot, which PersonPage draws after the first three in the order they are written here:
        the same ids and the same headings as the kit's slots, only present when there is something to say. */}
    {sameName.length > 0 && (
    <PersonSection kind="namesakes">
      <p class="small">These three towns repeat their names relentlessly — this
      archive holds four men called Rosario Mazza and four called Giuseppe Arena.
      <strong> These are different people, kept apart on purpose rather than merged into one.</strong>
      <a href={u("/method/")}> Why →</a></p>
      <ul style="margin:.8rem 0 0;padding-left:18px;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.8">
        {sameName.map((q) => (
          <li><a href={u("/people/" + q.slug)}>{q.name}</a>
            <span style="color:var(--ink-3)"> — {q.living ? "a living person"
              : ([q.born, q.died].filter(Boolean).join("–") || "undated")}
              {q.birthPlace ? `, ${q.birthPlace}` : ""}{q.living || q.comp == null ? "" : `, component ${q.comp}`}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </PersonSection>
    )}

    {/* NO IMAGE IS RENDERED LARGER THAN IT IS.
        This grid had width:100% in cells of at least 190px, so an image held
        at 132x161 was stretched past its own resolution and shown blurred.
        Angela Mazza's lead photograph was one: 132x161, 3,901 bytes, a
        complete and valid JPEG and simply small — MyHeritage held no more of
        it, and the signed links expired on 16 September 2026, so there is no
        better copy to fetch. Eleven of the archive's 133 images are under
        190px wide and every one of them was being upscaled.

        width:auto with max-width:100% is the whole fix: a large photograph
        still shrinks to its cell, and a small one is drawn at the size it
        actually is. A small photograph ought to look small. Pretending
        otherwise is the same habit as a sentence claiming more than the data
        holds, which is most of what this week's work has been about.

        NO PHOTOGRAPH OF A LIVING PERSON (16 October 2026): a living person has no photographs in people.json
        at all, the files themselves are withdrawn from the build, and the kit does not draw the slot. */}
    {!living && p.photos && p.photos.length > 0 && (
    <PersonSection kind="media">
      <div class="grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(190px,1fr))">
        {p.photos.map((f) => {
          const wh = (p.photoSize || {})[f];
          const small = wh && wh[0] < 190;
          return (
          <figure style="margin:0">
            <img src={u("/plates/" + f)} alt={p.name}
                 width={wh ? wh[0] : undefined} height={wh ? wh[1] : undefined}
                 loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                 style="border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:3px;display:block;width:auto;max-width:100%;margin:0 auto;height:auto" />
            <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:6px;text-align:center">
              {p.name}, from the family's own tree.
              {small && (<> Held only at {wh[0]}&times;{wh[1]}. <strong>A better scan would help.</strong></>)}
            </figcaption>
          </figure>);
        })}
      </div>
      <p class="cap">From the family's own tree, contributed by Michael Mazza and relatives. Almost
      none are captioned — <strong>identifications are the most valuable thing you can send.</strong></p>
    </PersonSection>
    )}

    {hasCorrections && (
    <PersonSection kind="notes">
      <div class="note" id="disputed">
        <strong>What a register says about this person, which the tree does not.</strong>
        {p.corrections.map((c) => <p style="margin-top:.5rem">{c}</p>)}
        <p style="margin-top:.5rem" class="small">Where the two disagree, <strong>the chart above
        still draws the tree as it stands</strong>. This archive does not silently rewrite what it
        was given — <a href={u("/scilla/")}>the acts are here</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </PersonSection>
    )}

    {/* The archive's own sections, drawn after the six the kit shares (kind="more": the page labels
        each «this archive only»). A living person has none: the kit does not draw the default slot for
        them, and `grave`, `fixes` and `ship` are null for them in any case. */}
    {p.registerNote && (
      <PersonSection kind="more" title="Why this person is here">
        <p class="small" style="white-space:pre-line" set:html={md(p.registerNote)} />
      </PersonSection>
    )}

    {grave && (
      <PersonSection kind="more" title="The burial">
        <div class="lab">Read from the register</div>
        <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
          <div class="k">Plot</div><div class="v"><strong>{grave.plot}</strong></div><div class="w">{grave.cemetery}</div>
          <div class="k">Born</div><div class="v">{grave.birth || "—"}</div><div class="w"></div>
          <div class="k">Died</div><div class="v">{grave.death || "—"}</div><div class="w"></div>
          <div class="k">Interred</div><div class="v">{grave.interment || "—"}</div><div class="w"></div>
          <div class="k">Entered as</div><div class="v">{grave.registerName}</div><div class="w"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">Brisbane Catholic Cemeteries deceased register, read 10 September 2026.
        <a href={u("/nudgee/")}> Nudgee →</a></p>
        {fixes.length > 0 && (
          <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px">The register <strong>corrects the family tree</strong>
          here: {fixes.map((c, i) => (<>{i > 0 && "; "}{c.field === "birth" ? "born" : "died"}
          {" "}<span style="text-decoration:line-through;color:var(--ink-3)">{c.was}</span> → <strong>{c.now}</strong></>))}.
          The site uses the register.</p>
        )}
      </PersonSection>
    )}

    {ship && (
      <PersonSection kind="more" title="The crossing">
        <div class="lab">Read from the passenger index</div>
        <div class="rec" style="margin-top:14px">
          <div class="k">Ship</div><div class="v"><strong><em>{ship.ship.charAt(0) + ship.ship.slice(1).toLowerCase()}</em></strong></div><div class="w">{ship.confidence}</div>
          <div class="k">Sailed from</div><div class="v">{ship.naaFrom}</div><div class="w"></div>
          <div class="k">Arrived</div><div class="v">{ship.naaPort}</div><div class="w">{ship.naaDate}</div>
          <div class="k">NAA item</div><div class="v"><a href={`https://recordsearch.naa.gov.au/scripts/AutoSearch.asp?Number=${ship.itemId}`} rel="noopener">{ship.itemId}</a></div><div class="w"></div>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">{ship.note} — National Archives of Australia, passenger arrivals index
        1898–1972, read 10 September 2026. <a href={u("/crossings/")}>The crossings →</a></p>
      </PersonSection>
    )}
  </PersonPage>
</Base>
